          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            %em Differential K-theory
            is the refinement of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+%28Eilenberg-Steenrod%29+cohomology"} generalized (Eilenberg-Steenrod) cohomology
            theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
          %p
            In as far as we can think of cocycles in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            as represented by
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "s," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectorial+bundle"} vectorial bundle
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
          %p There are various different models that differ in the concrete realization of these cocycles and in their extra properties.
          %h2#SimonsSullivanModel The Simons-Sullivan model
          %p
            This section discusses the model presented in (
            %a{:href => "#SimonsSullivan"}> SimonsSullivan
            ).
          %p More details will eventually be at
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Simons-Sullivan+structured+bundle"}> Simons-Sullivan structured bundle
              \.
          %h3#idea_2 Idea
          %p
            In the Simons-Sullivan model cocycles in differential K-theory are represented by ordinary
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} with connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concordance"} concordance
            such that the corresponding
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern-Simons form
            is exact.
          %h3#details Details
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \to X
            be a complex
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundle
            with
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            2-form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo ,
                  %mi End
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F = F_\nabla \in \Omega^2(X,End(V))
                  \\,.
          %p
            %strong Definition
          %p
            The
            %strong
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            is the inhomogenous
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature+characteristic+form"} curvature characteristic form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi ℕ
                  %msub
                    %mi k
                    %mi j
                  %mi tr
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo ∧
                  %mi ⋯
                  %mo ∧
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo •
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  ch(\nabla) := \sum_{j \in \mathbb{N}}
                  k_j
                  tr( F_\nabla \wedge \cdots \wedge F_\nabla)
                  \\;\;
                  \\in \Omega^{2 \bullet}(X)
                  \\,,
          %p
            where on the right we have
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} j
            wedge factors of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            \.
          %p
            %strong Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,\nabla)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ′
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V',\nabla')
            be two complex vector bundles with connection.
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern-Simons+form"} Chern-Simons form
            for this pair is a differential form
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mi ω
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi Ω
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo •
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CS(\nabla,\nabla') + d \omega \in
                  \\Omega^{2 \bullet + 1}(X)
          %p
            obtained from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concordance"} concordance
            bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar V \to X \times [0,1]
            given by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pullback"} pullback
            along
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ×
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \times [0,1] \to X
            equipped with a connection
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bar \nabla
            such that …, by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mo ∫
                    %mn 0
                    %mn 1
                  %msubsup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi t
                    %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ι
                    %mrow
                      %mo ∂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                      %mo ∂
                      %mi t
                  %mi ch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mo ∇
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo +
                  %mi d
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  CS(\nabla,\nabla') =
                  \\int_0^1 \psi_t^* (\iota_{\partial/\partial t} ch(\bar \nabla))
                  + d (...)
                  \\,.
          %p
            %strong Proposition
            This is indeed well defined in that it is independent of the chosen
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/concordance"} concordance
          %p
            %strong Definition
          %p
            A
            %strong structured bundle
            in the sense of the Simons-Sullivan model is a complex vector bundle
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
            equipped with the equivalence class
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [\nabla]
            of a connection under the equivalence relation that identifies two connections
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nabla'
            if their Chern-Simons form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi CS
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ∇
                  %mo ′
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CS(\nabla,\nabla')
            is exact.
          %p Two structured bundles are isomorphic if there is a vector bundle isomorphism under which the two equivalence classes of connections are identified.
          %p
            %strong Definition
          %p
            Let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Struc
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Struc(X)
            be the set of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            classes of structured bundles on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          %p
            Under
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+product"} tensor product
            of vector bundles, this becomes a commutatve
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig"} rig
          %p Let
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo :
                  %mo =
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Struct
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\hat K(X) := K(Struct(X))
          %p
            be the additive
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+completion"} group completion
            of this rig as usual in
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
          %p
            So as an additive group
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat K(X)
            is the quotient of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            induced by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
            on pairs
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,W)
            of isomorphism classes in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Struc
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Struc(X)
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,V)
          %p
            Hence the pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 0
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,0)
            is the additive inverse to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (0,V)
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo ,
                  %mi W
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (V,W)
            may be written as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo −
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V - W
          %p
            %strong Theorem
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi K
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ^
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hat K(X)
            is indeed a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            refinement of ordinary K-theory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi K
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K(X)
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            (i.e. of the 0th
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohomology+group"} cohomology group
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory+spectrum"} K-cohomology
          %p Moreover…
          %h2#the_bunkeschick_model The Bunke-Schick model
          %h3#idea_3 Idea
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Uli+Bunke"} Uli Bunke
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Schick"} Thomas Schick
            developed in a series of articles a differential-geometric cocycle model of differential K-theory where cocycles are given by smooth families of
            = succeed "s." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+operator"} Dirac operator
          %p
            See the reference
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "BunkeSchickReferences"} below
          %h3#properties Properties
          %p
            The restriction of the cocycles in the Bunke-Schick model to those whose “auxialiary form”
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            vanishes reproduces the Simons-Sullivan model above.
          %h2#the_hopkinssinger_model The Hopkins-Singer model
          %p See at
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+function+complex"} differential function complex
            %li
              %p
                %em
                  %a{:href => "differential+cohomology+diagram#HSDifferentialKU"} differential cohomology diagram – Hopkins-Singer differential KU
          %h2#more_models_in_smooth_spectra More models in smooth spectra
          %p
            See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "differential+cohomology+diagram#DifferentialKTheory"} Differential cohomology diagram – Differential K-theory
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              In
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gauge+theory"} gauge theory
              gauge fields are modeled by cocycles in
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/integral+transform"} integral transform
              acting on differential K-theory classes is
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/flat+K-theory"} flat K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"}> K-theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} topological K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %strong differential K-theory
              %ul
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+differential+K-theory"} fiber integration in differential K-theory
                %li
                  %p
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+K-theory+of+smooth+manifolds"} algebraic K-theory of smooth manifolds
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+K-theory"} twisted differential K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivariant+differential+K-theory"}> equivariant differential K-theory
                ,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orbifold+differential+K-theory"} orbifold differential K-theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+algebraic+K-theory"} differential algebraic K-theory
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p
            An early sketch of a general definition, motivated by the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dirac+charge+quantization+and+generalized+differential+cohomology"} Dirac charge quantization and generalized differential cohomology
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0011220"} arXiv:hep-th/0011220
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"} Michael Hopkins
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On Ramond-Ramond fields and K-theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0002027"} arXiv:hep-th/0002027
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/1126-6708/2000/05/044"} doi:10.1088/1126-6708/2000/05/044
          %p
            Then the general construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology"} differential cohomology
            theories via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+function+complexes"} differential function complexes
            of
          %ul
            %li#HopkinsSinger05
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Hopkins"}> Michael Hopkins
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Isadore+Singer"} Isadore Singer
              = succeed "," do
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quadratic+Functions+in+Geometry%2C+Topology%2C+and+M-Theory"} Quadratic Functions in Geometry, Topology, and M-Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math.AT/0211216"} arXiv:math.AT/0211216
          %p
            (motivated in turn by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/7d+Chern-Simons+theory"} 7d Chern-Simons theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-brane"} M5-brane
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
          %p provides in particular a model for differential K-theory.
          %p For more historical remarks see section 1.6 of
          %ul
            %li#FreedLott10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lott"} John Lott
              = succeed "," do
                %em An index theorem in differential K-theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~lott/gt-2010-14-021p.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            A discussion of more models and their relation in the context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cohomology+hexagon"} differential cohomology hexagon
            then appears in
          %ul
            %li#BunkeNikolausVoelkl13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Nikolaus"} Thomas Nikolaus
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+V%C3%B6lkl"} Michael Völkl
              %em Differential cohomology theories as sheaves of spectra
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1311.3188"}> arXiv:1311.3188
              )
          %p Survey:
          %ul
            %li#BunkeSchick10
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Schick"} Thomas Schick
              %em Differential K-theory. A survey
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1011.6663"}> arXiv:1011.6663
              ).
          %p
            Cocycle models via
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bundle+with+connection"} bundle with connection
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Max+Karoubi"}> Max Karoubi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Homologie cyclique et K-théorie
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/AST_1987__149__1_0"} numdam:AST_1987__149__1_0
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lott"}> John Lott
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                      %semantics
                        %mrow
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi R
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} /
                          %mstyle{:mathvariant => "bold"}
                            %mi Z
                        %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbf{R}/\mathbf{Z}
                    Index theory
                %strong 2
                2 (1994) 279-311 (
                %a{:href => "https://math.berkeley.edu/~lott/rztheory.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li#SimonsSullivan
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/James+Simons"}> James Simons
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Dennis+Sullivan"} Dennis Sullivan
                %em Structured vector bundles define differential K-theory
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0810.4935"}> arXiv:0810.4935
                )
          %p The basic article for the Bunke-Schick model is
          %ul
            %li#BunkeSchick09
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Schick"} Thomas Schick
              = succeed "," do
                %em Smooth K-Theory
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0707.0046"} arXiv:0707.0046
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.numdam.org/item/?id=AST_2009__328__45_0"} numdam:AST_2009__328__45_0
          %p A survey talk is
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
              ,
              %em Differential cohomology in geometry and analysis
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.uni-regensburg.de/Fakultaeten/nat_Fak_I/Bunke/Vortrag-Erlangen.pdf"}> pdf slides
              )
          %p
            On differential
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-theory"} KO-theory
          %ul
            %li#GradySati18
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Differential KO-theory: Constructions, computations, and applications
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1809.07059"} arXiv:1809.07059
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/j.aim.2021.107671"} doi:10.1016/j.aim.2021.107671
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Kiyonori+Gomi"}> Kiyonori Gomi
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mayuko+Yamashita"} Mayuko Yamashita
                %em Differential KO-theory via gradations and mass terms
                \&amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2111.01377"}> arXiv:2111.01377
                \&amp;rbrack;
          %p
            Of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-theory"} orthogonal
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} K-theory
          %ul
            %li#GradySati19
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %em Twisted differential KO-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09085"}> arXiv:1905.09085
              )
          %p
            The equivalence of these models with the respective special case of the general construction in
            %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger05"} Hopkins &amp; Singer 05
            in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+function+complexes"} differential function complexes
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              Kevin Klonoff,
              %em An Index Theorem in Differential K-Theory
              PdD thesis (2008) (
              %a{:href => "http://www.lib.utexas.edu/etd/d/2008/klonoffk16802/klonoffk16802.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p (assuming the existence of a universal connection, which is not strictly proven) and
          %ul
            %li
              Michael L. Ortiz,
              %em Differential Equivariant K-Theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0905.0476"}> arXiv:0905.0476
              )
          %p (not needing that assumption).
          %p
            A construction of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+cobordism+cohomology"} differential cobordism cohomology
            theory in terms of explicit geometric cocycles is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ulrich+Bunke"}> Ulrich Bunke
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Schick"} Thomas Schick
              %em Landweber exact formal group laws and smooth cohomology theories
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0711.1134"}> arXiv:0711.1134
              )
          %p
            By tensoring this with the suitable ring, this also gives a model for differential K-theory, as well as for
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+elliptic+cohomology"} differential elliptic cohomology
          %p
            A variant of this definition with the advantage that there is a natural morphism to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cheeger-Simons+differential+characters"} Cheeger-Simons differential characters
            refining the total
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+class"} Chern class
            is (as opposed to the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Chern+character"} Chern character
          %ul
            %li
              Alain Berthomieu,
              %em A version of smooth K-theory adapted to the total Chern class
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/PS_cache/arxiv/pdf/0806/0806.4728v1.pdf"}> pdf
              )
          %p
            Discussion for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/odd+Chern+character"} odd Chern character
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Tradler"}> Thomas Tradler
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Wilson"} Scott Wilson
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mahmoud+Zeinalian"} Mahmoud Zeinalian
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An Elementary Differential Extension of Odd K-theory
              %p
                and Topology, (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1211.4477"}> arXiv:1211.4477
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Wilson"}> Scott Wilson
                ,
                %em A loop group extension of the odd Chern character
                (
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1311.6393"}> arXiv:1311.6393
                )
          %p
            A model in terms of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+bundles"} super vector bundles
            is given in
          %ul
            %li
              Jae Min Lee, Byungdo Park,
              = succeed "," do
                %em A Superbundle Description of Differential K-Theory
              = succeed ")." do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.3390/axioms12010082"} doi:10.3390/axioms12010082
          %p
            Computation for the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+stack"} moduli stack
            of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/connection+on+a+bundle"} principal bundles with connection
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
              \.
              %em
                On the differential
                = succeed "-theory" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi K
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
              (2025). (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2501.03108"}> arXiv:2501.03108
              ).
          %h3#relation_to_index_theory Relation to index theory
          %p
            Relation to
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/index+theory"} index theory
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Kevin Klonoff,
                %em An Index Theorem in Differential K-Theory
                PdD thesis (2008) (
                %a{:href => "http://www.lib.utexas.edu/etd/d/2008/klonoffk16802/klonoffk16802.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Lott"} John Lott
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An index theorem in differential K-theory
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://math.berkeley.edu/~lott/gt-2010-14-021p.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            See also the references at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fiber+integration+in+differential+K-theory"} fiber integration in differential K-theory
          %h3#in_string_theory In string theory
          %p
            A survey of the role of differential
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi K
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} K
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+theory"} string theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Freed"}> Daniel Freed
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em K-Theory in Quantum Field Theory
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math-ph/0206031"} arXiv:math-ph/0206031
          %p
            The operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-duality"} T-duality
            on hypothetical
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+differential+K-theory"} twisted differential K-theory
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alexander+Kahle"}> Alexander Kahle
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alessandro+Valentino"} Alessandro Valentino
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/T-Duality+and+Differential+K-Theory"} T-Duality and Differential K-Theory
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} K-theory
            and its relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+string+theory"} type II string theory
            (see also
            = succeed "):" do
              %a{:href => "D-brane#ReferencesKTheoryDescription"} there
          %ul
            %li#GradySati19a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              = succeed "," do
                %em Ramond-Ramond fields and twisted differential K-theory
              %p
                Advances in Theoretical and Mathematical Physics
                %strong 26
                (2022) 5 &amp;lbrack;
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1903.08843"}> arXiv:1903.08843
                ,
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://dx.doi.org/10.4310/ATMP.2022.v26.n5.a2"} doi:10.4310/ATMP.2022.v26.n5.a2
          %p
            Discussion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twisted+K-theory"} twisted
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/differential+K-theory"} differential
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/KO-theory"} orthogonal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+K-theory"} K-theory
            and its relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/D-brane+charge"} D-brane charge
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+I+string+theory"} type I string theory
            (on
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orientifolds"} orientifolds
          %ul
            %li#GradySati19b
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Daniel+Grady"}> Daniel Grady
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hisham+Sati"} Hisham Sati
              %em Twisted differential KO-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1905.09085"}> arXiv:1905.09085
              )
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Thomas+Tradler"}> Thomas Tradler
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Scott+Wilson"} Scott Wilson
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mahmoud+Zeinalian"} Mahmoud Zeinalian
              %em Loop Differential K-theory
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1201.4593"}> arXiv:1201.4593
              )
        .revisedby
